---
/**
 * A breath between the capability grid and the exit, not another card: the whole
 * argument is at /learn/relay-with-governance, and one claim plus a text link is
 * the honest amount of it to put on the way in.
 */
---

<section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 pb-32">
    <div class="max-w-3xl border-l-2 border-emerald-500/40 pl-6 md:pl-9">
        <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-emerald-400 uppercase tracking-[0.14em]">
            Governed async tasks
        </span>
        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-100 mt-3 mb-4 text-balance">
            Your longest-running operations were the least governed ones
        </h2>
        <p class="text-lg text-zinc-400 mb-6">
            The export, the migration, the bulk write — everything expensive and hard to
            undo hands back a handle in milliseconds and does the real work later. Hangar
            governs the relay of that task, and the moment it pauses for input, without
            ever becoming the executor: no scheduler, no job runner, no result store of
            its own.
        </p>
        <a
            href="/learn/relay-with-governance"
            class="group inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-emerald-400 hover:text-emerald-300 transition-colors"
        >
            How the governed relay works
            <span class="transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-0.5" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </a>
    </div>
</section>
